 "result": "All of this is in `src/tessel/views.py`, in two classes: `View` and `VerbView` (which subclasses `View`).\n\n**Becoming a view function:**\n- `View.to_view_func(name, *args, **kwargs)` (L86) is a classmethod that builds a plain `view(**kwargs)` function.\n- On each request, `view` instantiates the class and calls `self.run_view(**kwargs)`, wrapped in `active_app.ensure_sync`.\n- If `init_every_request = False`, it reuses a single instance instead.\n- It applies `cls.decorators` and copies `view_class`, `methods` and `provide_automatic_options` onto the function, so route registration can read them.\n\n**Registering the allowed methods (`VerbView.__init_subclass__`, L165):**\n- If the subclass doesn't define `methods`, it collects `methods` from the base classes.\n- It then adds the upper-cased name of every method in `http_method_funcs` that the class defines (`get` becomes `GET`, `post` becomes `POST`).\n- The result becomes `cls.methods`, which the route rule uses.\n\n**Dispatching (`VerbView.run_view`, L182):**\n- It looks up `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`.\n- A `HEAD` request falls back to `get` if there is no `head` handler.\n- It calls the handler with the URL variables as keyword arguments.",
